# Cloudflare Pages response headers. THIS FILE IS THE CLOUDFLARE HALF OF
# vercel.json's "headers" block — the two hosts read different files, so a
# rule added to one and not the other ships on one host only.
#
# public/ is copied into the export verbatim, so this lands at out/_headers,
# which is where Pages looks for it. Pages does not serve /_headers itself.
#
# Syntax (Cloudflare's, not Vercel's JSON):
#   a line starting with "/" is a path rule; the INDENTED lines under it are
#   "Name: value" headers applied to it. "*" is a splat, one per rule, and it
#   spans path separators. "#" comments, blank lines ignored. Matching is on
#   the PATH only — a query string cannot be matched on.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The big downloads: ~20 MB of models, textures, audio and sky, and the whole
# reason this file exists. They are the same three prefixes vercel.json gives
# a one-year immutable lifetime, so a returning player pays for none of them
# twice. Without these lines every visit re-downloads or at best re-validates
# tens of megabytes, one round trip per file.
#
# Their filenames are STABLE across builds while their contents are not, which
# is normally the one case where "immutable" is unsafe. What makes it safe is
# lib/build.ts's buildStamped(): the game asks for /models/foo.glb?v=<build>,
# so the URL changes whenever the bundle does and a new deploy can never be
# answered out of the old entry. Read the note in lib/build.ts before
# shortening any of these.
#
# One deliberate difference from next.config.mjs: its rule is gated on the
# request carrying a `v` query, and a _headers rule cannot look at the query.
# vercel.json's rule is ungated too, and it is the one that runs in production
# today, so this matches what is live rather than widening it.
/models/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=31536000, immutable

/assets/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=31536000, immutable

/hdri/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=31536000, immutable

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The JS/CSS bundle. Not in vercel.json because it did not need to be: Next
# serves /_next/static with exactly this header on its own, and Vercel keeps
# that. A plain static host does not — Pages' default for an asset is
# revalidate-every-time — so without this line the move to Cloudflare would
# QUIETLY make the game slower to start than it is on Vercel. Every file under
# here is content-hashed by the build, so the name changes when the bytes do.
/_next/static/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=31536000, immutable
